  <s:String x:Key="Lint.BigCard">size {0} m, the particle always faces the camera</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.BigCard.Fix">such a particle is a flat card in the screen plane with no volume; several smaller ones with random rotation look fuller</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.BigCard.Name">Huge flat card</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.BirthGate">no births for {0} % of the effect time ({1} of {2} s)</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.BirthGate.Fix">this is how X-Ray builds up an effect; if it must show at once, flatten the "Births over effect time" graph</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.BirthGate.Name">Births off for most of the effect</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.BirthRateRnd">≈ {0} births/s on average in game, the viewport gives {1}</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.BirthRateRnd.Fix">the game adds about 0.73 of the spread, not half (three measurements, law not found)</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.BirthRateRnd.Name">Birth spread</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.CloseAlpha">closer than 2 m to alpha layers of other parts: {0} (e.g. {1})</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.CloseAlpha.Fix">the game orders layers by their source; with close sources they swap from frame to frame — move them apart or make glows additive</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.CloseAlpha.Name">Alpha layers of different parts close</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.DenseNoSort">dense cloud: ≈ {0} sprites at each point, "Sort particles" is off</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.DenseNoSort.Fix">without sorting the game draws particles in birth order — new ones cover near ones; switch sorting on (unless the source turns)</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.DenseNoSort.Name">Dense cloud without sorting</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.DonorPath">{0} lies in the donor folder "{1}"</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.DonorPath.Fix">this project's export rewrites the path under its own folder; a file used without the export keeps pointing at someone else's data</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.DonorPath.Name">Path into a donor folder</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.DonorTag">the name "{0}" carries a donor tag: {1}</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.DonorTag.Fix">release CG names carry only the CG tag; donor tags are temporary showcase marks</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.DonorTag.Name">Donor tag in the name</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.EmissiveSmoke">{0}: smoke or dust, additive, without a normal map</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.EmissiveSmoke.Fix">such smoke is not lit by the sun and glows at night; use an ordinary alpha material for smoke</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.EmissiveSmoke.Name">Smoke glows by itself</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.FarOffset">offset {0} m, particles in the world, the source turns</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.FarOffset.Fix">not reproduced in probes, but a cut at 15–22 m was reported; check in game or switch on "In source space"</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.FarOffset.Name">Far offset on a turning source</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.FarShimmer">{0} m is {1} px at {2} m, the source moves</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.FarShimmer.Fix">small moving particles blink far away (tornado debris); let them be born only near — "Only nearer, m" on the part (Anomaly panel)</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.FarShimmer.Name">Small particles shimmer far away</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.GlowAtNoon">additive glows in the part: {0}, bright dust nearby, the sky is noon</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.GlowAtNoon.Fix">at noon glows over bright dust are almost invisible, even in front; thin bright bolts do show. Check the dusk and night skies</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.GlowAtNoon.Name">Glow at noon over dust</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.GraphAboveOne">graph {0} has a key at {1} — the game clamps it to 1</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.GraphAboveOne.Fix">graphs are multipliers 0–1 (seen in game); raise the field itself and keep the graph within 1</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.GraphAboveOne.Name">Graph above 1</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.GraphUndershoot">curve {0} dips to {1} while its lowest key is {2}</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.GraphUndershoot.Fix">the game draws a smooth curve through the keys, and between close keys it dips below them (see the curve editor); spread the keys or add one</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.GraphUndershoot.Name">Curve dips</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.LitMoving">lit material {0} on a moving source</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.LitMoving.Fix">on the tornado the layer swaps showed as blinking, and the lighting cost +0.6 ms per frame; use flat dust for moving parts</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.LitMoving.Name">Lit material on a moving source</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.ManyDecimals">graphs with 4 or more decimals: {0}</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.ManyDecimals.Fix">if the file is saved in Workbench, it rounds every graph to 3 decimals; here values are kept as they are</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.ManyDecimals.Name">More than 3 decimals in graphs</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.MaterialMissing">material not found: {0}</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.MaterialMissing.Fix">the game will not load it and the emitter draws nothing; check the path and the data folders in the settings</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.MaterialMissing.Name">Material missing</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.MasterTail">the game reads this graph from 32 samples: {0} % of the drawn births never happen</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.MasterTail.Fix">the game reads 32 points of the graph and never what lies after 0.969 of the effect time; a window narrower than 1/31 of the effect time may vanish. Keep windows wider and end them earlier, or lengthen the effect time</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.MasterTail.Name">Births the game never gives</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.SampledGraph">the game reads graph {0} from 32 points: it differs from the drawn curve by {1} % of its value</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.SampledGraph.Fix">the game reads 32 points of this graph and never what lies after 0.969 of its time; a step becomes a ramp 1/31 wide and a narrower window may vanish (the curve editor shows the game's read dashed). Widen windows and end them earlier</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.SampledGraph.Name">Graph the game reads coarser</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.FrameLoss">at 60 frames per second the game gives {0} % fewer particles: {2} instead of {1}</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.FrameLoss.Fix">the game adds each frame's births one frame late and the frame that reaches the effect time gives none, so a short one-shot emitter loses its last one or two frames. Lengthen the effect time by a few frames and let MaxNum hold the count, or make the window longer</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.FrameLoss.Name">Short emitter loses its last frames</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.ColdCut">the first time a player sees this effect (a {2} ms loading frame) it gives {1} particles instead of {0}</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.ColdCut.Fix">only for a particle not registered in ParticleList: the game loads registered particles when it starts, then there is no load frame. Otherwise it loads the effect the first time it shows it, that frame is long, and a frame that reaches the effect time ends the births. Register the particle, or make the effect time longer than the load frame plus two frames (at most 0.3 s plus two frames) and let MaxNum hold the count</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.ColdCut.Name">Lost on the first show</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.MaxNumLow">needs ≈ {0} particles, "Max particles" is {1}</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.MaxNumLow.Fix">particles per second × life exceed the limit — the extra ones are never born and the cloud is thinner</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.MaxNumLow.Name">Maximum below the need</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.MixedStops">the part's hard stop is at {0} s, but its emitters end at different times: {1}</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.MixedStops.Fix">the stop removes every particle of the part at once; if the emitters must end at different times, give each its own part</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.MixedStops.Name">Different ends under one stop</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.NameChars">the name "{0}" has spaces or symbols</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.NameChars.Fix">Workbench does not accept such names; Latin letters, digits and "_" only</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.NameChars.Name">Odd characters in the name</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.NameKeyword">the name "{0}" is an Enforce keyword</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.NameKeyword.Fix">Workbench opens it unnamed and saves "EmitorDef {" — the effect breaks; rename it</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.NameKeyword.Name">Name is a language word</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.NameTaken">the name "{0}" is already in this part</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.NameTaken.Fix">Workbench refuses it and the live link finds emitters by name; rename it</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.NameTaken.Name">Name repeats</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.Nameless">the emitter has no name</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.Nameless.Fix">this is what Workbench saves after a keyword name; give it a name</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.Nameless.Name">Emitter without a name</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.NeverEmits">nothing shows: the field {0} keeps particles from appearing</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.NeverEmits.Fix">zero births, a limit of 0, a birth or transparency graph all at zero, or size 0; fix the field or remove the emitter</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.NeverEmits.Name">Emitter shows nothing</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.NoEnf1">{0}: .edds file without the ENF1 mark</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.NoEnf1.Fix">this is a plain DDS named .edds — the game shows garbage; save it again through the Atlas panel</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.NoEnf1.Name">No ENF1 mark</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.NpotDxt">{0}: compressed texture {1}×{2}, sides are not powers of two</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.NpotDxt.Fix">the game shows garbage blocks; rebuild with 2ⁿ sides or uncompressed in the Atlas panel</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.NpotDxt.Name">Compressed texture not a power of two</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.Restitution">"Bounce" {0} — in game the particles freeze where they are born</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.Restitution.Fix">no bounce was seen in game (measured); set 0</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.Restitution.Name">Bounce freezes particles</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.SizedShape">shape {0} with a non-zero "Shape size" — draws nothing in game</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.SizedShape.Fix">set the shape size to 0 or use an ellipse or box</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.SizedShape.Name">Cone or sphere with a size</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.SortOnSpin">"Sort particles" on a source turning {0}°/s</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.SortOnSpin.Fix">on the tornado it made the blinking worse; switch it off or stop the part's turn</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.SortOnSpin.Name">Sorting on a spinning cloud</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.Spring">"Pull to source" {0}</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.Spring.Fix">in game such a part moved differently from the viewport (live link, P11); check with a game frame</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.Spring.Name">Pull to the source</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.SpringLocal">"Pull to source" {0} on a part that turns with its source — in game the particles fly away</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.SpringLocal.Fix">in game 16.10 such particles vanished (at 0.05) or drifted away (at 0.000001); move the pull into a separate emitter without "In source space"</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.SpringLocal.Name">Pull on a turning part</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.StackedAxis">{0} alpha emitters stacked on one axis on a moving source: {1}</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.StackedAxis.Fix">they share one depth from the camera and swap every frame under a turn; one emitter at the base with growing puffs keeps the order</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.StackedAxis.Name">Stack on one axis</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.Streak">{0}: a streak material (ParticleStreak)</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.Streak.Fix">in game it drew nothing with the same keys as a working material; use an ordinary particle material</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.Streak.Name">Streak material draws nothing</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="Lint.TextureBig">{0}: a side of {1} px is over {2}</s:String>
